  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-213404",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e., $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- $10$ does *not* have a multiplicative inverse modulo $50$ since $\\text{gcd}(10,50)=10\\ne 1.\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- $33$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $50$ since $\\text{gcd}(33,50)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $45$ does *not* have a multiplicative inverse modulo $50$ since $\\text{gcd}(45,50)=5\\ne 1.\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
